    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Water-based polyurethane lost an argument it should have won years ago. The early formulations really were softer than oil, and the reputation stuck long after the chemistry moved on. Today the most durable floor finish you can buy in a can is water-based.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <h2 id="vs-oil" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. Water-Based vs Oil-Based
        </h2>

        <p>
          Four differences matter, and only one of them still favours oil.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Colour.</strong> Oil-based polyurethane ambers as it cures and keeps yellowing for years. On red oak that warmth is often welcome and part of the traditional look. On maple, ash, birch, white oak with a pale stain, or anything grey or white-washed, it is a slow disaster — the floor you chose is not the floor you end up with. Water-based dries clear and stays clear, which is the single biggest reason it now dominates new work.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Durability.</strong> A two-component water-based finish is harder and more scratch-resistant than oil-based polyurethane. A single-component one is broadly comparable. The old hierarchy is simply gone, and any advice that assumes otherwise is describing products from a previous era.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Dry time and odour.</strong> Water-based recoats in two to four hours against eight to twenty-four for oil, which turns a week-long floor project into a weekend. The odour difference is even starker: oil-based polyurethane makes a house genuinely unpleasant for days, while water-based is mild enough to live around.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Ease of application.</strong> This is oil's remaining advantage. It flows and levels slowly, so it forgives a hesitant applicator. Water-based sets fast and punishes stopping, going back over a tacky area, or working too slowly in a warm room. It is not difficult, but it demands that you keep moving.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
          <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers</p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2-4 h</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Between coats</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3-4</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Coats on a floor</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24 h</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Before light foot traffic</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">2-3 wk</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Before full cure and rugs</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="one-vs-two" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. One-Part vs Two-Part
        </h2>

        <p>
          This distinction matters more than the brand on the can, and most buyers never hear about it.
        </p>

        <p>
          A <strong>single-component</strong> water-based finish dries as water evaporates and the resin particles coalesce into a film. It is simple, forgiving of an interrupted job, and stores in the can until next time. Durability is good rather than exceptional.
        </p>

        <p>
          A <strong>two-component</strong> finish adds a cross-linking hardener, mixed in immediately before use. That hardener chemically bonds the film together as it cures, producing a substantially tougher, more chemical-resistant surface — the difference is obvious in a hallway after two years. The cost is a pot life: once mixed, the clock runs, and whatever you have not used is wasted.
        </p>

        <p>
          The practical rule: two-part for floors that get real traffic — entrances, hallways, kitchens, family rooms, rental properties. One-part for bedrooms, studies, furniture, trim and anything where the wear is light and the convenience of an open can matters more.
        </p>

        <!-- Product Reviews -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Water-Based Polyurethanes, Reviewed
          </h2>

          <p>
            We applied these to oak and maple test boards and to real floors and furniture, scoring how well they laid down, how clear they stayed, how the sheen held across coats, and how they looked after months of use. Scratch resistance was checked the way a house checks it — chair legs, dropped tools and a dog — rather than in a laboratory.
          </p>

