                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                  <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-4 border border-gray-100">
                    <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-1">The Bottom Line</p>
                    <p class="text-gray-900 font-bold">{product.bottomLine}</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Floors, Furniture and Everything Else</h3>
        <p>
          The label matters less than matching the product's intended scale of work. A dedicated <strong>floor finish</strong> is formulated for abrasion from foot traffic and grit, and it is what belongs on any floor that gets walked on with shoes. A <strong>furniture topcoat</strong> is formulated to lay down beautifully and resist household spills, which is a different specification — it will look better on a table top and wear through faster in a hallway.
        </p>
        <p>
          The overlap is real, though, and worth exploiting. A furniture-grade topcoat on stair treads, on a window seat or on a low-traffic bedroom floor gives a noticeably nicer surface than a floor product would, because it levels better and the sheen is more even. Conversely, a floor finish on a kitchen worktop or a workbench is a perfectly sensible piece of over-specification.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where people go wrong is using a light-duty topcoat on a busy floor because it was cheap and on the shelf. That is the single most common cause of a floor showing traffic lanes within a year, and it gets blamed on water-based chemistry generally rather than on a product being asked to do a job it was never sold for.
        </p>

        <h2 id="sheen" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          4. Choosing a Sheen
        </h2>

        <p>
          Sheen is a maintenance decision disguised as an aesthetic one. The glossier the finish, the more it reflects — and reflections show every scratch, dust particle and paw print.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Gloss</strong> looks spectacular for about a week in a real house. It magnifies every subsequent scuff, and on a floor it also shows the sanding scratches underneath more clearly. Reserve it for furniture or small showcase pieces.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Semi-gloss</strong> is the traditional floor sheen and still common, particularly where a period look is wanted. It shows wear more than satin but has real depth.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Satin</strong> is what most floors should be. It hides scratches, dust and traffic patterns better than anything shinier while still looking finished rather than flat. If you are unsure, this is the answer.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Matte</strong> is increasingly fashionable and hides wear best of all, at the cost of some of the depth that makes a wood floor look rich. It also shows spills and footprints more readily than satin in some formulations, so it is worth seeing a sample before committing a whole floor.
        </p>

        <p>
          One technical note: sheen comes from flattening agents suspended in the finish, and they settle. Stir thoroughly and often — never shake, which introduces bubbles — or the last coat will be glossier than the first for reasons that have nothing to do with your technique.
        </p>

        <h2 id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. Applying It Properly
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Prepare properly.</strong> On a bare or freshly sanded floor, work through the grits and finish at 100 to 120 — going finer burnishes the wood and can cause adhesion problems. Vacuum thoroughly, then tack off with a barely damp cloth. Dust is the single biggest cause of a disappointing floor, and it is entirely within your control.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Use the right applicator.</strong> A T-bar or synthetic pad on a pole for floors; a quality synthetic brush or foam applicator for stairs, trim and furniture. Never a natural-bristle brush — the bristles absorb water, swell and go limp. Our <a href="/best-brush-for-water-based-polyurethane/">brush guide</a> covers the options.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Work with the grain, keep a wet edge, and keep moving.</strong> Water-based sets fast. Plan your exit before you start, work in manageable strips along the boards, and never go back over an area that has begun to tack up — that is what produces the drag marks and lap lines people blame on the product.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Abrade between coats.</strong> A light pass with 220 grit or a fine screen, then vacuum and tack. The first coat raises the grain of bare wood noticeably, so that first abrade does the most work.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Three coats minimum, four in busy rooms.</strong> Water-based builds thinner per coat than oil, and the extra coat is where the durability comes from. On stairs and thresholds, an extra coat on those areas alone is cheap insurance.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Watch the conditions.</strong> Very warm rooms and moving air accelerate drying to the point where the finish sets before it levels. Close windows to stop draughts across the floor, and avoid the hottest part of the day in summer.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Much You Need</h3>
        <p>
          Coverage runs around 400 to 500 square feet per gallon per coat on a sanded hardwood floor, less on open-grained or freshly sanded wood that drinks the first coat. For a 300-square-foot room at three coats, that is roughly two gallons with sensible margin — and buying it in one purchase matters, because sheen and batch can vary slightly between production runs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Budget for the consumables too. A T-bar applicator, a fresh pad, 220-grit paper or screens for the between-coat abrades, tack cloths, and painter's tape add up to a meaningful fraction of the finish cost, and running out of any of them mid-floor is far more disruptive than it sounds when the clock on a wet edge is running.
        </p>

        <h2 id="curing" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. Drying vs Curing
        </h2>

        <p>
          Dry and cured are different states, and confusing them ruins more new floors than bad application does.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Dry</strong> means the water has evaporated and the surface can be recoated or lightly walked on — a few hours between coats, around 24 hours before socks on the floor.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Cured</strong> means the film has reached its final hardness, and that takes two to three weeks. During that window the finish is soft enough to be marked by furniture feet, gouged by a dragged appliance, and — most commonly — damaged under rugs, which trap moisture and prevent proper curing while leaving an outline you cannot remove.
        </p>

        <p>
          The practical schedule: no shoes for a couple of days, furniture back after about a week with felt pads under everything, and rugs after the full two to three weeks. Resisting the urge to put the room back together immediately is the cheapest thing you can do for a floor you have just spent a weekend on.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Looking After the Finish</h3>
        <p>
          A water-based floor finish is maintained mostly by keeping grit off it. Sand and grit tracked in from outside act as an abrasive underfoot and do more damage in a year than pets and furniture combined, so a doormat at every entrance and regular vacuuming with a hard-floor head is genuinely the main maintenance task.
        </p>
        <p>
          Clean with a product intended for polyurethane floors rather than a general household cleaner. Anything with ammonia, vinegar, oil soap or wax is a problem: acids dull the film, oils and waxes leave a residue that makes future recoating fail, and excess water sitting in the seams between boards eventually gets under the finish at the edges. A barely damp microfibre mop is all a sealed floor needs.
        </p>
        <p>
          When wear paths eventually appear in the traffic lanes — usually five to ten years on a well-applied two-component finish — the fix is a screen and recoat rather than a full sand. Abrading the existing finish and laying one fresh coat restores the surface in a day, provided the wear has not gone through to bare wood. Catching it at that stage is the difference between a day's work and a full refinish.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Going Back Over a Tacky Area</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water-based sets fast, and touching up a section that has started to tack drags the film and leaves marks that cannot be brushed out. Keep moving, keep a wet edge, and fix imperfections on the next coat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Natural-Bristle Brush</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Natural bristles absorb water, swell and lose their shape, leaving streaks and shed hairs in the finish. Synthetic bristles or a foam applicator are the only sensible options.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shaking the Can</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Shaking whips air into the finish and those bubbles end up in the film. Stir slowly and thoroughly instead, and keep stirring between coats so the flattening agents stay suspended and the sheen stays consistent.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting Rugs Down Too Early</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A rug on a finish that has not fully cured traps moisture, prevents hardening, and leaves an outline that does not come out. Two to three weeks, however tempting the empty room looks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Third Coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water-based builds a thinner film per coat than oil-based, so two coats is genuinely not enough on a floor. The third is where the durability lives, and the fourth is cheap insurance in a hallway.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best water-based polyurethane for floors?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Bona Traffic HD is the most durable available, because the two-component chemistry cross-links far more thoroughly than single-component finishes. For a homeowner doing their own floor, Varathane's Ultimate Floor Finish balances durability, price and availability best.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it as durable as oil-based?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Two-component water-based finishes are more durable than oil-based polyurethane, and single-component products are broadly comparable while drying faster and staying clear. The belief that water-based is inherently softer describes formulations that are decades out of date.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does it yellow over time?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No — and that is its main advantage. Oil-based ambers as it cures and keeps yellowing for years, which warms oak and visibly discolours maple, ash and white-washed floors. Water-based dries clear and stays clear.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats do floors need?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Three as standard, four in a hallway or kitchen. Water-based builds a thinner film per coat than oil, so the extra coat is not optional — two coats of water-based is not the equivalent of two coats of oil.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to dry?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Two to four hours between coats, which is why a whole floor can be done in a day. Curing is separate: light traffic after 24 hours, furniture after about a week with felt pads, and full hardness at two to three weeks. Rugs wait until then.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you apply it over oil-based?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, if the oil finish is fully cured — usually at least 30 days — and the surface is scuff-sanded and cleaned so the new coat has a mechanical key. Over a finish that is still curing, adhesion can fail, so test a corner if you are unsure.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What applicator is best?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">A T-bar or synthetic pad on a pole for floors, because they lay an even film down quickly. A synthetic brush or foam applicator for stairs, trim and furniture. Never natural bristle — it absorbs water and goes limp.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you sand between coats?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes — a light pass with 220 grit or a fine screen knocks down raised grain and keys the next coat. The first coat on bare wood raises the grain most, so that abrade matters more than the later ones. Vacuum and tack off thoroughly afterwards.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

